# SYNOPSIS

```usage
trace seconds dir [hz n] [pages n] [category ...]
```

# DESCRIPTION

`trace` records the kernel's samples of what each CPU runs for <seconds>, then copies the recording to <dir>: `kernel.trace`, the kernel's trace (trace(6)), and `procs`, the processes it saw and where their code is mapped.
The words after <dir> are proc(4)'s `start` command's: `hz` the samples a second (1000 by default), `pages` how much each CPU keeps (512 pages, 65,536 records, by default), and each <category> the kernel's other records to keep as well (`sched`, `irq`, `vm`, `ipc`, `futex`, `syscall`).
It makes <dir> if it isn't there.

It is an rc script over `/proc/trace/ctl`: it writes `start` and its words, prints `trace: recording`, sleeps (sleep(1)), writes `stop`, copies the two files and prints `trace: copied to <dir>`.

In a cpu(8) session <dir> is typically under `/mnt/term`, on the dev machine, where `vx trace profile` (vx-trace(1)) names the samples from the build and counts them by process and by function; `vx remote trace` (vx-remote(1)) does both in one step.

# EXAMPLES

From a cpu session, two seconds at 4 kHz, then on the dev machine:

```rc
% trace 2 /mnt/term/root/prof hz 4000
trace: recording
trace: copied to /mnt/term/root/prof
```

```rc
% vx trace profile $home/prof
```

